AB  - This study reconceptualizes stewardship in nonprofit relationship building by examining how distinct stewardship practices foster organizational identification among donors and volunteers through perceived relationship investment and perceived social impact. Building on Kelly’s stewardship framework, the study proposes a four-dimensional model consisting of personalized communication, personalized acknowledgment, impact reporting, and engagement opportunity. Survey data from 429 nonprofit stakeholders show that donors were more responsive to impact reporting and engagement opportunity, whereas volunteers were more responsive to personalized communication and personalized acknowledgment. Perceived relationship investment and perceived social impact significantly mediated the effects of stewardship on organizational identification, while cause fit and social recognition strengthened these relationships. The study contributes to stewardship theory by identifying psychological mechanisms and stakeholder-contingent effects within a contemporary, digitally mediated nonprofit context. Practically, the findings suggest that nonprofit organizations should adopt role-sensitive stewardship strategies rather than one-size-fits-all relationship approaches.
